On Fri, 3 Mar 2006 pauls2272@yahoo.com wrote:
>
[Arthur wrote:]
>> This patch allows monsters to pick up and use certain kinds of rings
>
> Does this mean that the ring has to be identified before the mob will
> try it on?

   No, monsters "magically" know the type of every item in the game. This 
is why it's possible to identify wands of digging, invisibility, etc., by 
watching what monsters do with them. (And, sad to say, wands of death. ;)
So the ring patch continues this tradition; monsters know what all the
rings are, even if you don't, and you can infer things about rings by 
watching monsters use them (if you're very patient!).

> Since mobs can wear rings can pets wear rings too (assuming they have
> fingers)?

   I believe so, yes. I think it's all handled by the same code.


   BTW, I just uploaded a new version based on L's and ManaUser's comments:
http://www.contrib.andrew.cmu.edu/~ajo/free-software/nethack/monring343.html
     * Generalized "purple worm" to "digester" in a few places.
     * Added monster-on-monster effects of slow digestion.
     * Monsters prefer fire resistance when you're wielding Fire Brand,
       and cold resistance when you're wielding Frost Brand.
     * Monsters' rings fall off with their gloves, when they're
       polymorphed into handless or tiny creatures.
     * Monsters can put on rings "over" gloves or other rings.
     * Uniques and shopkeepers can wear rings of regeneration.

   Re the fourth point above: It looks like a handless monster /can/ wear 
a ring (but not gloves), if it's the player polyed into that kind of 
monster. I don't understand this, but I've left it in. However, in the
new version of the patch, polying a mob into, say, a fog cloud, makes it
drop its armor /and/ any ring it was wearing.

